(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2026-005"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2026-005","title":"Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","sourceTitle":"Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2026","num":5,"category":"basic","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/010_Basic Level Test-1 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>n</em>! = 1 × 2 × 3 × _ _ _ × <em>n</em> for integer <em>n</em> ≥ 1. If <em>P</em> = (1 × 1!) + (2 × 2!) + (3 × 3!) + ______ (10 × 10!),<br />\nthen <em>P</em> + 2 when divided by 11! leaves a remainder of</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10"},{"key":"B","html":"0"},{"key":"C","html":"7"},{"key":"D","html":"1"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b10b4e74da14e267b1d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Δ<em>ABC</em> and Δ<em>DBC</em> have a common base and drawn towards the same side, ∠<em>BAC</em> = ∠<em>BDC</em> = 90°. If <em>AC</em> and <em>DB</em> intersect at <em>P</em>, then</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23fbbd45d36d44282961.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>PC</em> = <em>BP</em> × <em>PD</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>BP</em> = <em>PC</em> × <em>PD</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>AP</em> × <em>PD</em> = <em>PC</em> × <em>BP</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea8923f9ebbc34b13419.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/6476af87eaa76cb40b73.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>the possible value(s) of <em>x</em> is/are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/def93987ae5c3340d6f4.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa950fe5f9a688a263c5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"3"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/3382afef9792c32123a4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f3664e511f282d80f70.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1"},{"key":"B","html":"2"},{"key":"C","html":"0"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>abc</em>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fcd72e8c92adccd0568e.png\" /></div>\n</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In the adjoining diagram, <em>ABCD</em> is a square with side ‘<em>a</em>’ cm. The area of the larger circle with centre ‘<em>O</em>’ is equal to the sum of the areas of the remaining four circles with equal radii, whose centres are <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em> and <em>S</em>. What is the ratio between the side of square and radius of a smaller circle?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c732cfa2820c208d4ef2.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7a29ce11e1655e673857.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/92/92bbdc422eeec47d1571.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c6e8f87f4bd66e10dbde.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"Can’t be determined"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e1c820ea2314a4f9fd8e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fcf9552703912e92c8a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The question below consists of a question and two statements numbered I and II given below it.<br />\nYou have to decide whether the data provided in the statements are sufficient to answer the question. Read both the statements and give answer<br />\n<em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em>, <em>S</em> and <em>T</em> are sitting around a circular table facing towards the centre. Who among them is sitting to the immediate right of <em>R</em>?<br />\nI. <em>Q</em> is sitting between <em>P</em> and <em>T</em>, and <em>T</em> is sitting to the immediate right of <em>S</em>.<br />\nII. <em>Q</em> is second to the left of <em>R</em> and is second to the right of <em>S</em>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The data in statement I alone is sufficient to answer the question, while the data in statement II alone is not sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"B","html":"The data in statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question, while the data in statement I alone is not sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"C","html":"The data either in statement I alone or in statement II alone is sufficient to answer the question;"},{"key":"D","html":"The data given in both statements I and II together are not sufficient to answer the question."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/871e8683fe34a8f03981.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If the <em>p</em><sup>th</sup> term of an <em>AP</em> is <em>q</em> and its <em>q</em><sup>th</sup> term is p, then its mth term is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91ee822ad75a6176e0da.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/beb3b38c63bfc9d31a15.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d8bb279f95e8283ac807.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1502feb64c379d5c853c.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f3fe35f73ef7acb2212.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In the following question, a part of the sentence is given in bold. Choose the correct alternative that best improves the bold part. If no improvement is needed, select “No improvement.”<br />\nThe Vice Chancellor congratulated the blind student <strong>for his</strong> success in the examination.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"in his"},{"key":"B","html":"on his"},{"key":"C","html":"over his"},{"key":"D","html":"No improvement"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe verb &ldquo;congratulate&rdquo; is typically followed by the preposition &ldquo;on&rdquo; when referring to the reason for the congratulation.<br />\nCorrect sentence: &ldquo;The Vice Chancellor congratulated the blind student on his success in the examination.&rdquo;</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Read the passage carefully and choose the best answer to the following question from the four alternatives given below.<br />\nIn the past 50 years, doctors across the world have accepted the practice to prescribe antibiotics at the first sign of a trivial infection or treat patients with a handful of antibiotics. These days it is not uncommon to see practitioners prescribing multiple antibiotics without any real indication or relevance for such a combination of drugs. Antibiotics have traditionally been known as miracle drugs, but there is growing evidence that they are overworked miracles, especially in countries like ours where there is easy access to drugs across the counter, including antibiotics. We cannot think of a return to pre-antibiotic days. Yet the unbridled use of these agents is inexorably propelling us in that direction.<br />\nAntibiotics are called overworked miracles because:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"they performed miracles."},{"key":"B","html":"they are hardly used."},{"key":"C","html":"they are over-used."},{"key":"D","html":"they exhausted their miracles."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe passage clearly points out that antibiotics are being excessively used, so they are called overworked miracles.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>For the mirror represented by a line MN, choose the correct mirror-image of the figure, from amongst the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given along with it.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c27179bb94658d842a48.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/9691568d1f87f20e414e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1e/1e507a01ad33df7415a7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/aba2e751240b17b5a74e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9a64d7a92572c7cf729b.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A random variable <em>X</em> is uniformly distributed in the range [–1, 2]. Another random variable <em>Y</em> is related to <em>X</em> as, <em>Y</em> = 2<em>X</em> + 3. The probability, <em>P</em>(<em>Y</em> ≤ 3) is equal to ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b2/b2d3e5dc70cf6317786f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0aa584793e623ff45405.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64e6a306dc5770bbeadb.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bd/bd96285a9dd8cd6ee79f.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c380274840da3cf0fe1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A matrix <em>A</em> is given as <em>A</em> = <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef52f2905749fd8de4c3.png\" /> then trace of <em>A</em><sup>102</sup> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e15a76c7ebe3d33229cf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following postfix expression.<br />\n10 2 3 4 ∗ + 5 – / 6 2 + ∗<br />\nEvaluate the result of the expression and determine the maximum stack depth during the evaluation process. (Assume integer arithmetic for all operations)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6, 5"},{"key":"B","html":"8, 4"},{"key":"C","html":"12, 4"},{"key":"D","html":"9, 5"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/3711cee5fed6ac476467.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Six dice are thrown 729 times. The expected number of times that atleast three dice shows a 5 or 6</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[233,233],"answerText":"233","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>233</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/40/40823fecbe33cfd3d5af.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/043ebe82756e3f699786.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f6/f6af36d08257b116215a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe20389ea67d2d955be2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/600bb43584d74a9b8ab6.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c1528f5635e93d2c26d6.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d7a7cb6790107dd5f5c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the English statement:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/66b559572461d9334ade.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following propositional formulas correctly represents the statement?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2ac50d8a7a319a3b7914.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2d/2d484faa4960020d2a7c.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/842cbfe5353342a8389d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cf/cfd9b5284af5b1b01783.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4f738b37bb701b2f25b0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the points (1, 1), (2, 2), (3, 1). Using the model <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/8c/8ccb6f5d1fe0517a20a0.png\" /> determine the optimal slope <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e6/e6e7a05c201372aa4ff6.png\" /> using the least squares criterion.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c8/c89f2a13e533b34d5044.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/8829d0b4eeb4f6eaa7ee.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb9f012c200efaff1f7b.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e0433123c7f1cf38e0b1.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6a3535a22832310b5a39.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Suppose the branching factor of a search tree is <em>b</em>, and Beam Search is applied with beam width k.<br />\nThe maximum number of nodes stored at any depth level is:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>b<sup>k</sup></em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>k</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>b</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>b</em> + <em>k</em>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nBeam width k limits the number of nodes kept at each depth, independent of branching factor.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the relation shown in the following table:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/3644cf7a759025901b7b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following FD’s are being satisfied by the above given instance of a relation?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>AB</em> → <em>C</em> and <em>AC</em> → <em>B</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>AB</em> → <em>C</em> and <em>BC</em> → <em>A</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>BC</em> → <em>A</em> and <em>AC</em> → <em>B</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following python code below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6dd3df5ddc95b9e35fd8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What will the output of the above code?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3, 3"},{"key":"B","html":"4, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"3, 2"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Arjun tries repeatedly to log into an online exam portal until he is successful. If there is 25%.<br />\nChance of logging in successfully in any attempt, then the average number of attempts that Arjun needs to make to log in successfully is ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2.5"},{"key":"B","html":"3.5"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c3c5a056d833e4489e1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59130814df7de4449884.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>For an Ο(log<sub>2</sub> log<sub>2</sub> n) algorithm, X takes 22 seconds to executes the given data, Y runs the same algorithm on same machine and it took 33 seconds for data size of 256, the data size of <em>X</em> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[16,16],"answerText":"16","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>16</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54d21bb8880362db607b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Two independent random variables <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are uniformly distributed in the range [0, 8] and [0, 4] respectively. Another random variable Z is defined as Z = X + Y.</p>\n\n<p>The variance of Z, that is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bbb7398debcc894d54dd.png\" /> is equal to ________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,7],"answerText":"6.66","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6.66 [6.00 - 7.00]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af8abd9a0d4ad6602e44.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Average linkage clustering defines the distance between two clusters as the mean of all pairwise distances.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Since it uses average distances, average linkage is less influenced by outliers compared to single or complete linkage.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; In average linkage clustering, the distance between two clusters is computed as the average<br />\nof all pairwise distances between points in the two clusters. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Because it takes an average rather than just minimum or maximum, it is less affected by<br />\nextreme values or outliers compared to single linkage (chaining effect) and complete linkage<br />\n(sensitive to farthest points). So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following array index start with 0.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/72/72b557031b04f19f5a43.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>To search an element <em>E</em>.<br />\nAverage number of comparison made by linear search = <em>A</em><br />\nAverage number of comparison made by binary search = <em>B</em><br />\nValue of <em>A</em> + <em>B</em> = ________.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8.5"},{"key":"B","html":"9.5"},{"key":"C","html":"11.5"},{"key":"D","html":"10.5"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f58cee0ed575fa2af91.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Assume queue ‘Q’ has the entries <em>p</em>, <em>q</em> and <em>r</em> (with <em>p</em> on front and r on rear). Initially stack ‘R’ is empty. An entry dequeue out of queue ‘Q’ can be printed immediately or pushed to stack R. An entry popped out of stack R can only be printed. The maximum number of permutations of output sequences that can be obtained in such a way that it either start or end with p are ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/8229bb46a2e803bdf82c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2e/2e3f2510e57c7c57c8b0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following set <em>F</em> of functional dependencies for relation <em>r</em>(<em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>, <em>D</em>, <em>E</em>):</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f51590a88d4a2d88787.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following highest normal form satisfied by <em>F</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 NF"},{"key":"B","html":"2 NF"},{"key":"C","html":"3 NF"},{"key":"D","html":"BCNF"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d2c7a99f53822e0ece58.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A Binary Search Tree (BST) stores values in the range 37 to 573. Consider the following sequences of keys.<br />\nI. 81, 537, 102, 439, 285, 376, 305<br />\nII. 52, 97, 121, 195, 242, 381, 472<br />\nIII. 142, 248, 520, 386, 345, 270, 307<br />\nIV. 550, 149, 507, 395, 463, 402, 270<br />\nWhich of the following statements is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I, II and IV are inorder sequences of three different BSTs."},{"key":"B","html":"I is a preorder sequence of some BST with 439 as the root."},{"key":"C","html":"II is an inorder sequence of some BST where 121 is the root and 52 is a leaf."},{"key":"D","html":"IV is a post order sequence of some BST with 149 as the root."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46d2dc3a46e267d1817c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a large sample test concerning the population mean, the hypothesized value under <em>H</em><sub>0</sub> is 4<sub>0</sub>. The population standard deviation is know to be 5, and the sample size is 16. If the calculate value of the test statistics under the standard normal approximate is 3.2, determine the sample mean that would be consist with this test statistics.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"36"},{"key":"B","html":"44"},{"key":"C","html":"48"},{"key":"D","html":"38"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2a4b260fe910cb9bfda8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following relation schema:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d9fa2739a8052072a191.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Query 1 and Query 2 is equal <em>R</em> ∩ <em>S</em> result"},{"key":"B","html":"Query 1 and Query 3 is equal <em>R</em> ∩ <em>S</em> result"},{"key":"C","html":"Query 2 and Query 3 is equal <em>R</em> ∩ <em>S</em> result"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/1263fed3398d46d077b6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Sigmoid activation suffers more from the vanishing gradient problem than ReLU."},{"key":"B","html":"ReLU may lead to the dying ReLU problem, where neurons output zero for all inputs."},{"key":"C","html":"The chaining effect occurs because merging is based on the minimum distance between clusters."},{"key":"D","html":"ReLU can saturate for large negative values, leading to inactive neurons."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)<br />\n&bull; Sigmoid activation compresses inputs into (0, 1), making gradients very small for large values<br />\n&rarr; vanishing gradient problem. ReLU avoids this. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Dying ReLU means some neurons output zero permanently, not all. The statement exaggerates,<br />\nso it is false. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; In single linkage clustering, merging is based on minimum distance &rarr; leads to the chaining<br />\neffect. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; ReLU does not saturate like sigmoid/tanh. For negative inputs, it just outputs 0 by design,<br />\nnot due to saturation. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":32,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let p, q and r be propositions and the expression:<br />\n(<em>p</em> ∨ <em>q</em> ∨ <em>r</em>) ∧ (¬<em>p</em> ∨ ¬<em>q</em> <em>∨</em> ¬<em>r</em>) is true for given conditions. Then which of the following is/are correct for given conditions.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"All three variables are true."},{"key":"B","html":"One of the variable is true while other two has false truth value."},{"key":"C","html":"One of the variable is false while other two has true truth value."},{"key":"D","html":"All three variables are false."}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)<br />\n(p &or; q &or; r) &and; (&not;p &or; &not;q &or; &not;r) is true when at least one of p, q and r is true and atleast one is false, but<br />\nis false when all three variables have the same truth value.</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e5f2143730d0e9db8d6.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>If <em>PQ</em> = <em>R</em> then determinant of matrix <em>P</em> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-14,-14],"answerText":"-14","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>14</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b3cec14c168ef4375fc4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following training dataset:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fc/fcb07c519fb3cdd495a5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Calculate the precision based on the classifier predictions.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.25"},{"key":"B","html":"0.45"},{"key":"C","html":"0.85"},{"key":"D","html":"0.75"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7f81c1d483b667532c7a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A producer of ball bearings knows that 4% of bearing are defective. Bearing are packed in cartons of 150. The producer guarantees that not more than 8 bearing will be detective in a carton. The approximate probability that a carton will fail to meet the guaranteed quality is ________. (Upto 3 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.15,0.16],"answerText":"0.152","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.152 [0.150 - 0.160]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/871d78e12f0d6a213351.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> Min-Max normalization is less sensitive to outliers compared to Z-score normalization.<br />\n<strong>S<sub>2</sub> :</strong> Min-Max normalization changes the distribution shape of the original data to a standard normal distribution.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider a hash table size of 11 that uses separate chaining for collision resolution. The hash function used is:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ec/ece5ef285baa5970bef4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Consider the following statements:<br />\n(i) The average chain length in the hash table is greater than 2 for non empty slots.<br />\n(ii) The index with maximum chain length is 6.<br />\n(iii) The are exactly seven chains and four empty slots in the hash table.<br />\n(iv) The total number of collision during key insertion is 11.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 4"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2"},{"key":"C","html":"3 and 4"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59aeb7e807628731cbb3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":38,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A node in a decision tree contains 100 samples. Among these, 40 samples belong to Class A, 35 samples belong to Class B, and 25 samples belong to Class C. Calculate the entropy of this node ________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.5,1.6],"answerText":"1.55","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.55 [1.50 - 1.60]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/5068f800a2535faef03f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A system using three level static indexing method to store a file. Block size is 512 bytes, block pointer and record pointer size are 20 bytes for each, search key size is 12 bytes. Base level block contains <search key, record pointer>, where record pointer contains address of block which<br />\ncontains record corresponding to search key.<br />\nAnd other two levels contains <search key, block pointers>.<br />\nOn accessing a record using search key, how many maximum possible search key comparison required to get the record pointer? (Assume top level have only one block and key search using sequential method).</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[48,48],"answerText":"48","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>48</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51f3fb255f17ded91e0c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong>S<sub>1</sub> :</strong> Suppose there are n elements in the range [1 + <em>n</em><sup>2</sup>]. Then counting sort performs better than selection sort in the worst case.<br />\n<strong>S<sub>2</sub> : </strong>Selection of pivot element decides the time complexity of quick sort.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nAs for the given range of elements, counting sort time complexity will be same as selection sort time complexity in worst case.<br />\nSo, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Gibbs Sampling can suffer from slow mixing when the variables in the distribution are highly correlated.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Gibbs Sampling produces independent samples at each iteration without any correlation.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; When variables are highly correlated, Gibbs updates move slowly in the sample space, leading<br />\nto slow mixing and higher auto correlation. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Gibbs Sampling generates a Markov chain, and consecutive samples are correlated, not independent. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>&nbsp;is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The value of the integral <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a1/a10dfabe065a4579f0c3.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64db88fa01a33a1905dc.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ed/edb7f34c8e204f8025dd.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c24b935ecfbe73233192.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/58f8b3bb333379cf0aae.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0b333631a49b2f8dbac8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>In the context of using LDA for classification when the number of features exceeds the number of samples, cross-validation is mainly used to:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Split data into folds to estimate generalization error."},{"key":"B","html":"Reduce the number of features before applying LDA."},{"key":"C","html":"Guarantee that LDA finds a perfect separating hyperplane."},{"key":"D","html":"Optimize and validate the performance of the model on unseen data."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)<br />\n&bull; Cross-validation allows us to repeatedly train/test on different splits to estimate error.<br />\nSo, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Incorrect. Dimensionality reduction methods like PCA reduce features, not cross-validation.<br />\nSo, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; LDA does not guarantee a perfect separating hyperplane. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Cross-validation validates how well the model generalizes and helps tune parameters.<br />\nSo, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9ab505b4060d57ad2142.png\" /> then probability P(simultaneous occurrence of event <em>E</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>E</em><sub>2</sub>) = ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/82872813e6c13ec107e7.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c71662e1f409e53cc52.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d8eec513793ae13330d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b497a546d91cd600a15.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5cd0d9cc29bca33b4806.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Bubble sort operates at the same speed whether the input is already sorted or in reverse sorted order.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Quick sort operates at equal speed whether the input is already sorted or in reverse sorted order.<br />\nWhich of the above is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The probability density function of random variable ‘<em>X</em>’ is defined as:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/00/00987778b3d37785f0ee.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The cumulative distribution function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e77b655f7e6e744c612.png\" /> is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.35"},{"key":"B","html":"0.11"},{"key":"C","html":"0.24"},{"key":"D","html":"0.38"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7dd5d9223aa7b8c09464.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Beam search gives the optimal solution.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : BFS takes more memory space compare to the UCS.<br />\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only preorder traversal of a binary tree is given, then we can construct unique binary tree."},{"key":"B","html":"Inorder traversal of BST is always sorted order."},{"key":"C","html":"Search time complexity of an element in AVL tree Ο(log <em>n</em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4fd387d023a230a16c2c.png\" /><br />\nFind the absolute maxima value of f(x) in the closed interval [0, 3] is ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"12"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"-5"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46025fcf4f803c39f14e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a binary max heap tree T with n number of nodes and <em>n</em> ≥ 2. Maximum possible level of a node in T is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8ec45e29b07e92388cae.png\" /> Level of second smallest element node <em>p</em> in T is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f2/f216848b2e42877ef100.png\" /> – 1. Which of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>n</em> must be even"},{"key":"B","html":"node <em>p</em> has atleast 1 child"},{"key":"C","html":"All levels of T can be full"},{"key":"D","html":"node <em>p</em> cannot have two children"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f8dd2c17549c1551e636.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The vectors <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/48844e74dcf1b864cb33.png\" /> are linearly independent if and only if</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"They are independent for all real values of <em>r</em> and <em>s</em>."},{"key":"B","html":"They are independent only when <em>r</em> ≠ 0 and <em>s</em> ≠ 3."},{"key":"C","html":"They are dependent if <em>r</em> = 0, but independent for all <em>r</em> ≠ 0."},{"key":"D","html":"They are dependent if <em>s</em> = 3 regardless of the value of <em>r</em>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/df/dfe25e17f0bc753388f7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If a heuristic is consistent, the f-values along any path are non-decreasing."},{"key":"B","html":"If two admissible heuristics <em>h</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>h</em><sub>2</sub> are given, then <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>) = max(<em>h</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>n</em>), <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>n</em>)) is also admissible."},{"key":"C","html":"If a heuristic is admissible, it may still overestimate the true cost in some cases."},{"key":"D","html":"All admissible heuristics are consistent."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4a/4a17f20037b16a015fb0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/11/113053dedb04b3204e77.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"MLPs consist of an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer."},{"key":"B","html":"MLPs can only solve linearly separable problems."},{"key":"C","html":"Non-linear activation functions like ReLU or sigmoid are used in hidden layers."},{"key":"D","html":"MLPs are trained using backpropagation."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)</p>"},{"n":54,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The following key values are inserted into an empty B+ tree where each node have two key values. The sum of keys present at height one (assume root is present at height 0) is ______.<br />\n(Use left biasing)<br />\n8, 5, 1, 7, 3, 12, 9, 6</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[18,18],"answerText":"18","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>18</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28f04cb4ea49aab0219e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>For an SVM with weight vector <em>W</em> = [7, -2] and input point x = [1, 4] the decision function value f(x) is 5. Find the bias term b.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"5"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d6/d6b8794eff9b37fdc962.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider three algorithms <em>A</em>, <em>B</em> and <em>C</em> with the following time complexities:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/808f8549ca8ad34abd72.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statement(s) are true?</div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>A</em>(<em>n</em>) grows faster than <em>B</em>(<em>n</em>) but slower than <em>C</em>(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9e/9efc813c2e7ede81c3f5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>B</em>(<em>n</em>) grows slower than both <em>A</em>(<em>n</em>) and <em>C</em>(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/24/249758832968b506124f.png\" />"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a4/a410d5017b8387cbd95f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is the correct decomposition of the directed graph given below into its strongly connected components?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a6408367eda1a8e40fdc.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"{P, Q, R, S} {T} {U} {V}"},{"key":"B","html":"{P, Q, R, S, T, V} {U}"},{"key":"C","html":"{P, Q, S, T, V} {R} {U}"},{"key":"D","html":"{P, Q, R, S, T, U, V}"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27df853c80e64e0ca6a8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following observations about vectors in <em>R</em><sup>(<em>n</em>)</sup>:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Two vectors are dependent whenever one of them can be expressed as a scalar multiple of the other.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If we take n vectors in <em>R</em><sup>(<em>n</em>)</sup> and none of them is a scalar multiple of another, then the set must be linearly independent.<br />\nWhich of the above is/are valid?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is valid"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is valid"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are valid"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is valid"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the system of linear equation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73a2dcff07548c94b0b8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If the <em>p</em> + <em>q</em> + <em>r</em> = 0, then the system of equation has</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Unique solution"},{"key":"B","html":"No solution"},{"key":"C","html":"Trivial solutions"},{"key":"D","html":"Infinitely many solutions"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1572d9442a4726e45f93.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following undirected graph G:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a2457880928c437cee8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are correct for minimum spanning tree of <em>G</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8 different MST possible for G."},{"key":"B","html":"Edge (G, C) is an edge in every possible MST."},{"key":"C","html":"Using Kruskal’s MST algorithm, order of edges included in one possible MST is (A, D), (B, E), (C, E), (E, F), (A, G), (C, G)."},{"key":"D","html":"(F, E) is included in every possible MST of G."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba4c20e02246affe2ce3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"OLAP systems often use star and snowflake schemas for efficient query performance."},{"key":"B","html":"OLTP systems are optimized for fast, atomic, real-time transaction processing."},{"key":"C","html":"Data in OLAP systems is always real-time and write-intensive."},{"key":"D","html":"Star Schema contain all type of data."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)<br />\n&bull; OLAP databases are structured in star/snowflake schemas because they support<br />\nmultidimensional queries and provide efficient query performance for analysis. So, option (a)<br />\nis correct.<br />\n&bull; OLTP (Online Transaction Processing) handles day-to-day operations (banking transactions,<br />\nreservations, retail purchases) with ACID properties ensuring correctness. So, option (b) is<br />\ncorrect.<br />\n&bull; OLAP is designed for historical, aggregated, read-intensive data analysis. It is not real-time<br />\nor write-intensive. So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; A star schema only contains fact tables (numerical data) and dimension tables (descriptive<br />\nattributes). It does not contain &ldquo;all types&rdquo; of data. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The chance that doctor <em>A</em> will diagnose a disease <em>X</em> correctly is 60%. The chance that a patient will die by his treatment after correct diagnosis is 40% and the chance of death by wrong diagnosis is 70%. A patient of doctor <em>A</em>, who had disease <em>X</em>, died then the chance that his disease was diagonised correctly is ________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.4,0.5],"answerText":"0.46","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.46 [0.40 - 0.50]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6fb7aaf0cc9d56fcbff6.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider we have given two large integers in binary format each of length n bits. What is the worst case time complexity to find product of two integers?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(<em>n</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>1.59</sup>)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73f44053134d542b9cbf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The Chi-square distribution is symmetric for all degree of freedom."},{"key":"B","html":"As the degree of freedom increases, the Chi-square distribution approaches a normal distribution."},{"key":"C","html":"The Chi-square test is used to estimate population mean when the variance is unknown."},{"key":"D","html":"Chi-square distribution only work for non-negative values."}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)<br />\n&bull; The Chi-square distribution is positively skewed, especially for small degrees of freedom. It becomes approximately symmetric only when degrees of freedom are large, but never perfectly symmetric. So, option (a) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; For large degrees of freedom, the Chi-square distribution tends toward a Normal distribution with mean df and variance 2 &sdot; df. So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n&bull; That is the role of the t-test, not Chi-square. The Chi-square test deals with variances and categorical frequency data (goodness-of-fit, independence, homogeneity). So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Because it is a sum of squares of standard normal variables, the Chi-square distribution cannot take negative values. So, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":65,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>For 3 × 3 matrices M and N, which of the following statement(s) is/are not correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"N<sup>T</sup> MN is symmetric or skew-symmetric, according as M is symmetric or skew-symmetric."},{"key":"B","html":"MN – NM is symmetric for all symmetric matrices M and N."},{"key":"C","html":"MN is symmetric for all symmetric matrices M and N."},{"key":"D","html":"(adj M) (adj N) = adj(MN) for all invertible matrices M and N. (adj stands for adjoint)"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03a3284b821af97bec88.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":42,"MSQ":12,"NAT":11},"withoutKey":0}};
